[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://manual_page_def.gd" id="1_page"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_page")
id = &"sight"
title = "What you can see"
order = 90
summary = "Three states of ground, and why armies are not remembered."
body = "Every squad and every building of yours sees a circle around itself. The
union of those circles is what you know.

The ground has three states, and both the world and the minimap show all
three:

- BLACK — never seen. You do not know what is there.
- DIM — seen once, not now. You know the lie of the land, and where the buildings were.
- LIT — in sight this second. This is the only place you see ARMIES.

## Armies are not remembered; buildings are

When an enemy squad leaves your sight it is gone from your screen — not
faded, not left where it was. A position you can see but cannot trust is
worse than none, because you would commit to it.

A building you have seen stays on your map until you watch it come down. The
difference is on purpose: an army is a thing that moves, and a building is a
thing you learned.

## Allies share sight

Everything your ally sees, you see. That is most of what an alliance is
mechanically, and it is a great deal — an ally scouting a flank is your
scout, at no cost to you.

## Your troops route around ground YOU have found

A squad sent across country you have never scouted takes the shortest line it
has no reason to doubt, and finds out by walking. It does not steer round the
lake nobody has seen. That is not the game being unhelpful; your army knows
exactly what you know, and no more."
sources = Array[String](["res://decisions/D-025-closes-d-004-s-provisional-semantics.md", "res://decisions/D-106-the-ground-has-fog-of-war.md", "res://decisions/D-20260818-pathing-knows-only-what-the-player-knows.md", "res://vision.gd"])
